  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    An ester links an acyl group to an alkoxy group through –COO–, the ester linkage.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The alkyl part comes from the alcohol (ethyl) and the acid part from the acid (ethanoate).
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The acid and the alcohol join with the loss of water, which is esterification.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The acid catalyses the reaction and absorbs the water, which pulls the equilibrium towards the ester.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Most simple esters are volatile and fruity, which is why they are used as flavourings and perfumes.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A triglyceride has three fatty acid chains esterified on to the three –OH groups of glycerol.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A cis double bond bends the chain, so the molecules cannot pack closely and the melting point is lower.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Hydrogen adds across the double bonds over a nickel catalyst at about 200 °C, straightening the chains.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Nickel catalyses the addition of hydrogen to the C=C bonds.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The alkali hydrolyses the ester links — saponification — giving the sodium salts of the fatty acids.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Saponification is the alkaline hydrolysis of the ester bonds of a fat.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The glycerol backbone is set free as propane-1,2,3-triol when the three ester links are broken.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The carboxylate group is ionic and dissolves in water; the long hydrocarbon tail is hydrophobic.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The tails bury in the grease and the heads face the water, so the grease is emulsified as micelles that are rinsed away.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ ions precipitate the soap as an insoluble calcium or magnesium salt, which is the scum.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The sulphonate head forms a soluble calcium salt, so no scum is produced.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Bacteria cannot easily break the branched chain, so the detergent persists and produces foam; straight-chain detergents replaced them.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Phosphates fertilise algae; when they die and decay, the oxygen in the water is used up.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Each monomer needs two reactive groups so that it can join at both ends and build a chain.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The –OH of one monomer and the –H of the other leave together as water; with acid chlorides, HCl is lost instead.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The amine and the acid join through an amide linkage, –CO–NH–, with the loss of water.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A diol and a dicarboxylic acid join through –COO–, the ester linkage.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The diol supplies the –O– half and the diacid the –CO– half of each ester link.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    PVC is made by opening the double bond of chloroethene; nothing is eliminated.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Styrene is phenylethene, CH₂=CHC₆H₅; its polymer is polystyrene.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Sulphur forms bridges between the polymer chains, which makes the rubber stronger and elastic.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Natural rubber is poly(isoprene), built from 2-methylbuta-1,3-diene units.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Bakelite is heavily cross-linked, so it cannot be softened and remoulded by heating.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    No common organism has an enzyme to break the carbon backbone, so the material remains for centuries.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The chlorine in the polymer is released as HCl, which is corrosive and must be scrubbed from the flue gas.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    One mole of acid gives one mole of ester; M(C₄H₈O₂) = 88, so 0.20 × 88 = 17.6 g.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The acid liberates carbon dioxide from the carbonate; the ester has no acidic hydrogen and gives no gas.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Alkaline hydrolysis gives glycerol and the sodium salts — the soap — rather than the free fatty acids.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The calcium salt of soap is insoluble and forms scum; the calcium salt of a sulphonate detergent is soluble.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It is the number of repeat units, exactly as for an addition polymer, though each unit is smaller than the two monomers combined.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Nothing is eliminated in addition polymerisation, so the percentage composition, and the empirical formula, are unchanged.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A protein is a condensation polymer of amino acids, joined by amide (peptide) bonds.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Water is not added as a reagent, but the alkali supplies the –OH that splits each ester bond: it is alkaline hydrolysis.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A surfactant concentrates at the oil–water boundary and lowers the interfacial tension, so the grease can be lifted.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Nylon is a polyamide made from a diamine and a dicarboxylic acid, with water lost at every link.
  ]

]

